Helper: initialise MemoryHeap::alignment.

HelperDeviceMemoryAllocator::MemoryHeap's constructor initialises size and type but not alignment.
GCC 13 at -O2 and above reports it as -Wmaybe-uninitialized (the member is copied in
FindOrCreateHeap before the first explicit store it can see), and NRI compiles with -Werror, so
the Linux build fails with the distribution compiler of Ubuntu 24.04. Upstream's own Ubuntu job
does not see it: Scripts/Linux/1-Deploy.sh configures without CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE, so GCC runs
unoptimised and the analysis never runs. Clang does not warn. Measured on v190 with GCC 13.3.0:
with this line the native build is clean and the test suite passes (docs/findings.md 8.1).

One initialiser, declaration order kept. Applies on top of NRI v190 with patches 0001 to 0004.

diff --git a/Source/Shared/HelperInterface.hpp b/Source/Shared/HelperInterface.hpp
index 1d54f9d..f367500 100644
--- a/Source/Shared/HelperInterface.hpp
+++ b/Source/Shared/HelperInterface.hpp
@@ -383,6 +383,7 @@ HelperDeviceMemoryAllocator::MemoryHeap::MemoryHeap(MemoryType memoryType, const
     , textures(stdAllocator)
     , textureOffsets(stdAllocator)
     , size(0)
+    , alignment(0)
     , type(memoryType) {
 }
 
